winewayland: mirror win32 z-order for promoted OWNED windows (gated, default off)

Bug A of the 11.10-64 regression: a captioned owned window promoted to a
subsurface SINKS behind the Program Monitor, and menus inside it sink behind it
in turn. No style discriminator exists -- the plugin windows and the extension
panel are the same class -- so narrowing the promotion cannot fix it.

The existing win32-z-order mirror never saw these windows. Phase A walks
GW_CHILD and an owned window is not a child; phase B only matched
client_surface, and a promoted owned window's subsurface is its MAIN
wayland_surface. Nothing ever restacked them, so a fresh wl_subsurface started
on top and then stayed wherever later siblings left it.

TWO MEASURED CORRECTIONS before this worked, both caught by insisting the code
prove it ran rather than assuming:

  1. The mirror itself is behind NEUTRON_NATIVE_STACK, off by default. With only
     NEUTRON_OWNED_ZORDER=1 a live Premiere session logged 347 promotions and
     ZERO restack lines: the fix was inert. Either gate now arms both phases.

  2. Discovery by GW_OWNER matched NOTHING -- **GW_OWNER is NULL for exactly
     these windows**, which the promotion code's own comment states, and why it
     falls back to owner_hint. Measured as 84 restack calls, 1 applied surface,
     the promoted window absent every time. Discovery now comes from the
     driver's own tree via wayland_surface->toplevel_hwnd, which is the
     relationship the driver already records.

Owned windows are appended LAST = top of the bottom-to-top order, the Win32 rule
that an owned window floats above its owner and the owner's children. The tree
walk is under win_data_mutex and makes no user-lock calls, respecting the
round-34 lock-order fix.

DEFAULT OFF (NEUTRON_OWNED_ZORDER=1). Verified on a live session that 1281
promotions produce zero protocol errors and no compositor disconnect, so the
fatal place_above hazard does not materialise -- but whether the plugin window
actually stops sinking is a visual result only the operator can confirm.

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/window.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/window.c
@@ -121,6 +121,37 @@
 static struct rb_tree win_data_rb = { wayland_win_data_cmp_rb };
 
 /***********************************************************************
+ *           wayland_owned_subsurfaces_of
+ *
+ * NEUTRON: the promoted OWNED windows currently parented to `toplevel`, from the driver's own
+ * tree. Caller MUST hold win_data_mutex; this makes no user-lock calls, so it is safe in the
+ * restack phase-B context.
+ *
+ * ⛔ Why not walk Win32 by GW_OWNER instead: **GW_OWNER is NULL for exactly these windows.** The
+ * promotion in WAYLAND_WindowPosChanged says so in its own comment and falls back to `owner_hint`
+ * for that reason -- owner_hint being a value win32u hands us, not something a later walk can
+ * recover. A first attempt at this fix looked them up by GW_OWNER and therefore matched NOTHING:
+ * measured on a live Premiere session as 84 restack calls, 1 applied surface, and the promoted
+ * window absent from it every time. The driver already records the relationship as
+ * wayland_surface->toplevel_hwnd, so ask that instead of re-deriving it.
+ */
+UINT wayland_owned_subsurfaces_of(HWND toplevel, HWND *out, UINT max)
+{
+    struct wayland_win_data *d;
+    UINT n = 0;
+
+    RB_FOR_EACH_ENTRY(d, &win_data_rb, struct wayland_win_data, entry)
+    {
+        if (n >= max) break;
+        if (d->hwnd == toplevel) continue;
+        if (!d->wayland_surface || !d->wayland_surface->wl_subsurface) continue;
+        if (d->wayland_surface->toplevel_hwnd != toplevel) continue;
+        out[n++] = d->hwnd;
+    }
+    return n;
+}
+
+/***********************************************************************
  *           wayland_win_data_create
  *
  * Create a data window structure for an existing window.
--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/waylanddrv.h
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/waylanddrv.h
@@ -559,6 +559,7 @@
 
 struct wayland_win_data *wayland_win_data_get(HWND hwnd);
 struct wayland_win_data *wayland_win_data_get_nolock(HWND hwnd);
+UINT wayland_owned_subsurfaces_of(HWND toplevel, HWND *out, UINT max);
 void wayland_win_data_release(struct wayland_win_data *data);
 
 struct wayland_client_surface *get_client_surface(HWND hwnd);
--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c
@@ -2275,6 +2275,29 @@
     return v;
 }
 
+/* NEUTRON: mirror win32 z-order for PROMOTED OWNED WINDOWS too, not just child client surfaces.
+ *
+ * Bug A of the 11.10-64 regression (build tester 2026-08-27): a captioned owned window promoted to
+ * a subsurface SINKS behind the Program Monitor, and menus inside it sink behind it in turn. The
+ * cause is that this mirror never saw those windows -- phase A walks GW_CHILD, and an owned window
+ * is not a child; phase B only matches `client_surface`, and a promoted owned window's subsurface
+ * is its MAIN wayland_surface. Missed on both counts, so nothing ever restacked them: a fresh
+ * wl_subsurface starts on top and then stays wherever later siblings leave it.
+ *
+ * In Win32 an owned window always floats above its owner and the owner's children, so they belong
+ * at the TOP of the bottom-to-top list.
+ *
+ * ⛔ DEFAULT OFF. This edits the most heavily-tuned path in the driver, and a place_above against
+ * a non-sibling is a FATAL protocol error (compositor disconnect = the app is killed), which is
+ * strictly worse than the bug. It ships off so it can be A/B'd on the machine that can actually
+ * see the symptom -- there are no VST plugins on the dev box and z-order is not visible in a log. */
+static BOOL neutron_owned_zorder(void)
+{
+    static int on = -1;
+    if (on < 0) { const char *e = getenv("NEUTRON_OWNED_ZORDER"); on = (e && e[0] == '1') ? 1 : 0; }
+    return on;
+}
+
 /* phase A: build the bottom-to-top win32 order of a toplevel's tree.
  * USER-LOCK calls only -- must be called WITHOUT win_data_mutex held. */
 static void neutron_zorder_collect_rec(HWND hwnd, struct neutron_zlist *zl, const POINT *origin)
@@ -2354,6 +2377,7 @@
     struct wayland_win_data *tdata = wayland_win_data_get_nolock(toplevel);
     struct wayland_surface *tsurf = tdata ? tdata->wayland_surface : NULL;
     struct wayland_client_surface *cs_of[NEUTRON_ZMAX];
+    struct wayland_client_surface owned_slots[NEUTRON_ZMAX];  /* see the owned-window branch below */
     BOOL below[NEUTRON_ZMAX];
     HWND applied[NEUTRON_ZMAX];
     struct wl_surface *prev;
@@ -2402,6 +2426,29 @@
         if (zl->hwnd[i] == toplevel) top_idx = (int)n; else child_clients++;
         n++;
     }
+    /* NEUTRON: append the promoted OWNED windows LAST = top of the bottom-to-top order, which is
+     * the Win32 rule (an owned window floats above its owner and the owner's children). Sourced
+     * from the driver's tree because GW_OWNER is NULL for them -- see
+     * wayland_owned_subsurfaces_of(). */
+    if (neutron_owned_zorder())
+    {
+        HWND owned[NEUTRON_ZMAX];
+        UINT no = wayland_owned_subsurfaces_of(toplevel, owned, NEUTRON_ZMAX), k;
+        for (k = 0; k < no && n < NEUTRON_ZMAX; k++)
+        {
+            struct wayland_win_data *od = wayland_win_data_get_nolock(owned[k]);
+            if (!od || !od->wayland_surface || !od->wayland_surface->wl_subsurface) continue;
+            memset(&owned_slots[n], 0, sizeof(owned_slots[n]));
+            owned_slots[n].wl_subsurface = od->wayland_surface->wl_subsurface;
+            owned_slots[n].wl_surface = od->wayland_surface->wl_surface;
+            owned_slots[n].toplevel_wl_surface = tsurf->wl_surface;
+            cs_of[n] = &owned_slots[n];
+            applied[n] = owned[k];
+            below[n] = FALSE;
+            n++;
+        }
+    }
+
     /* ...plus (a) the toplevel's OWN client surface when it hosts >=1 child client
      * subsurface (black container signature; belt-and-braces -- z-order alone
      * already puts content children above it). */
@@ -2536,7 +2583,13 @@
     /* NEUTRON round-35 native stacking, phase A (user-lock queries, PRE-lock):
      * collect the toplevel's win32 z-order so phase B can mirror it in the
      * wl_subsurface stack without any user-lock call under win_data_mutex. */
-    if (neutron_native_stack() && toplevel && visible)
+    /* NEUTRON: OWNED_ZORDER needs this mirror to actually run. Measured 2026-08-27 on a live
+     * Premiere session: with only NEUTRON_OWNED_ZORDER=1 the log carried 347 owned-window
+     * promotions and ZERO restack lines -- the whole phase A/B path sits behind
+     * NEUTRON_NATIVE_STACK, which is off by default, so the owned-window fix was inert. Nearly
+     * shipped a fix that provably does nothing; the "prove the flag reached the code" rule is
+     * what caught it. Either gate now arms the mirror. */
+    if ((neutron_native_stack() || neutron_owned_zorder()) && toplevel && visible)
         neutron_zorder_collect(toplevel, &zl);
 
     if (((neutron_dbg_n++) & 0xFF) == 0)
@@ -2627,7 +2680,7 @@
         /* NEUTRON round-35 native stacking, phase B (under win_data_mutex, NO
          * user-lock calls): mirror the collected win32 z-order in the
          * wl_subsurface stack (order cache no-ops when unchanged). */
-        if (neutron_native_stack() && zl.count)
+        if ((neutron_native_stack() || neutron_owned_zorder()) && zl.count)
             neutron_restack_apply(toplevel, &zl);
     }
     else if (neutron_keepclient() && toplevel && parent_visible)
